AIMED.سجّل({"slug":"respiratory-system","عنوان":"Respiratory System","تمهيد":"","أقسام":[{"معرّف":"first-symptoms-of-the-respiratory-system","عنوان":"First: Symptoms of the Respiratory System","html":"<p>A symptom is a subjective experience reported by the patient. The respiratory system generates a wide array of symptoms, each arising from specific pathophysiological mechanisms involving the airways, lung parenchyma, pleura, pulmonary vasculature, chest wall, or central respiratory control centers.</p><ul><li><strong>Cough:</strong> A forced expulsive maneuver against an initially closed glottis, generating high intrathoracic pressure followed by sudden glottic opening and explosive release of air. It is a defensive reflex triggered by stimulation of irritant receptors located in the larynx, trachea, bronchi, and even the external auditory canal (Arnold&#39;s nerve). Afferent impulses travel via the vagus nerve to the cough center in the medulla oblongata. The mechanism of action of the reflex arc involves deep inspiration, glottic closure, contraction of expiratory muscles (abdominal and intercostal) to build pressure up to 300 mm Hg, and sudden glottic opening, producing high-velocity expiratory airflow that clears mucus, foreign material, and noxious substances. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Dyspnea (Breathlessness):</strong> An uncomfortable awareness of the act of breathing. Pathogenesis is complex and multifactorial, involving an imbalance between central respiratory motor command (efferent-reafferent dissociation) and sensory feedback from chemoreceptors (PaO₂, PaCO₂, pH), mechanoreceptors in the chest wall and lungs (stretch and irritant receptors), and J-receptors in the pulmonary interstitium. When the perceived effort of breathing (work of breathing) is disproportionate to the achieved ventilation, dyspnea results. This mismatch is processed in the insular cortex and anterior cingulate gyrus. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a></li><li><strong>Hemoptysis:</strong> Expectoration of blood originating from the lower respiratory tract (below the vocal cords). The pathophysiology involves disruption of the bronchial or pulmonary vasculature. The bronchial arteries (systemic, high-pressure) are the source in the vast majority of cases (e.g., bronchiectasis, bronchitis, tumors), whereas the pulmonary arteries (low-pressure) are involved in conditions like pulmonary embolism with infarction or left heart failure with elevated pulmonary venous pressure. Massive hemoptysis is typically defined as exceeding 200-600 mL of blood expectorated in 24 hours, with mortality more closely related to asphyxiation from airway flooding than exsanguination. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a></li><li><strong>Chest Pain of Respiratory Origin (Pleuritic Pain):</strong> Sharp, stabbing, knife-like pain that is exacerbated by deep inspiration, coughing, or sneezing. Pathogenesis involves inflammation or irritation of the parietal pleura, which is richly innervated by the intercostal nerves (T1-T12) and the phrenic nerve (C3-C5). The visceral pleura is insensitive to pain. The pain is therefore localized to the site of pleural irritation and often referred along the course of the nerve (e.g., diaphragmatic pleural irritation via the phrenic nerve causes referred pain to the ipsilateral shoulder tip, C4 dermatome). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li><li><strong>Wheezing:</strong> A continuous, high-pitched, musical adventitious lung sound heard predominantly during expiration but also inspiration in severe narrowing. It is generated by the oscillation of apposed airway walls as airflow velocity increases through narrowed segments, obeying the Bernoulli principle. The airway narrowing arises from bronchospasm (smooth muscle contraction), mucosal edema, intraluminal secretions, or extrinsic compression. The pitch is proportional to the degree of narrowing. Monophonic wheeze (single note) suggests a single large airway obstruction, while polyphonic wheeze (multiple pitches) indicates diffuse small airway narrowing, most classically in asthma. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-5\" data-مصادر=\"5\">[5]</a></li><li><strong>Stridor:</strong> A harsh, high-pitched, monophonic, predominantly inspiratory crowing sound produced by turbulent airflow through a significantly narrowed segment of the extrathoracic upper airway (larynx or trachea). It indicates a critical reduction in airway caliber (typically to less than 5 mm in the adult larynx) and constitutes a medical emergency. Inspiratory stridor implies a supraglottic or glottic lesion, expiratory stridor implies an intrathoracic tracheal lesion, and biphasic stridor implies a fixed subglottic or tracheal stenosis. The mechanism is the same as a wheeze (airway wall oscillation due to Bernoulli effect), but the site and severity are distinct. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a></li><li><strong>Sputum Production:</strong> The expectoration of material from the lower airways. Normal healthy individuals produce about 100 mL of mucus daily from goblet cells and submucosal glands, which is cleared by the mucociliary escalator and swallowed unconsciously. Visible sputum signifies disease that has caused hypersecretion of mucus, sloughing of epithelial cells, and infiltration of inflammatory cells (neutrophils, eosinophils) or leakage of plasma proteins. Purulent sputum (yellow-green) is due to myeloperoxidase released from neutrophils, not necessarily bacterial infection. Mucoid sputum is clear and gelatinous; it typifies chronic bronchitis and asthma. Foul-smelling sputum suggests anaerobic infection (lung abscess, aspiration pneumonia). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Hoarseness (Dysphonia):</strong> Alteration in voice quality, pitch, volume, or effort. Although not primarily a respiratory symptom, it frequently coexists because the larynx is the conduit for air. Pathogenesis involves any disruption to the vocal fold mucosal wave, including inflammation (laryngitis), mass lesions (nodules, papillomas, carcinoma), recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis (surgical injury, malignant infiltration of the left RLN in the aortopulmonary window), or muscle tension imbalance. A hoarse voice persisting longer than three weeks without an acute upper respiratory infection requires laryngoscopy to exclude malignancy. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-7\" data-مصادر=\"7\">[7]</a></li><li><strong>Snoring and Apnea:</strong> Snoring is a harsh inspiratory noise generated by vibration of the soft palate, uvula, pharyngeal walls, and tongue base during sleep when pharyngeal dilator muscle tone is reduced and the upper airway becomes floppy. Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) occurs when pharyngeal collapse is complete, causing cessation of airflow (apnea) for &gt;10 seconds despite persistent respiratory effort, leading to oxyhemoglobin desaturation, sympathetic surge, and arousal from sleep, which restores airway patency. This cycle repeats, fragmenting sleep architecture and causing daytime somnolence and cardiovascular morbidity. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\">[8]</a></li></ul><div class=\"كتلة دليل\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Guideline currency (Rule 8a)</p><p>The current documents are the <strong>GINA 2026 Strategy Report</strong> (asthma) and the <strong>GOLD 2026 Report</strong> (COPD); both were confirmed to exist at the date in the verification footer [WEB]. Where this file&#39;s treatment text predates them, the guideline outranks the file.</p></div>"},{"معرّف":"second-signs-of-the-respiratory-system","عنوان":"Second: Signs of the Respiratory System","html":"<p>A sign is an objective finding detected by the clinician. In respiratory medicine, signs arise from abnormalities in ventilation, perfusion, diffusion, or the structural integrity of the chest wall and pleura.</p><ul><li><strong>Central Cyanosis:</strong> A bluish-purple discoloration of the mucous membranes (lips, tongue) and skin, reflecting an increased concentration of deoxygenated hemoglobin (&gt;5 g/dL) in the capillary blood. Pathogenesis: It is not a sign of reduced PaO₂ per se, but of severe hypoxemia (PaO₂ typically &lt; 45-50 mm Hg, equivalent to SaO₂ &lt; 80-85%) or a hemoglobin abnormality (methemoglobinemia, sulfhemoglobinemia). Central cyanosis implies an intrapulmonary shunt, severe ventilation-perfusion mismatch, or right-to-left cardiac shunt; it is absent in anemia because the absolute mass of deoxyhemoglobin is insufficient, and it is masked in polycythemia where it appears at higher PaO₂. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Peripheral Cyanosis:</strong> Bluish discoloration confined to the extremities (fingers, toes, nail beds, tip of nose). Pathogenesis: Vasoconstriction and stasis in the peripheral capillary bed, leading to increased oxygen extraction by tissues and a high arteriovenous oxygen difference in a setting of normal systemic arterial saturation. It is a sign of low cardiac output, cold exposure, or Raynaud phenomenon, not primarily of lung disease. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Clubbing (Hippocratic Fingers):</strong> Bulbous enlargement of the distal phalanges with loss of the normal Lovibond angle (angle between nail plate and proximal nail fold, usually &lt;165°), increased nail bed sponginess (fluctuation), and development of a &quot;drumstick&quot; appearance. Pathogenesis: The dominant mechanism is megakaryocyte impaction in the digital capillary beds. Normally, large platelet precursor cells (megakaryocytes) are fragmented in the pulmonary microcirculation. In conditions causing right-to-left shunting or direct bypass of the pulmonary filter (e.g., intrapulmonary vascular dilatations in hepatopulmonary syndrome, Eisenmenger syndrome, bronchogenic carcinoma releasing growth factors like VEGF and PDGF), intact megakaryocytes enter the systemic circulation, become impacted in the fingertip capillaries, and release platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), inducing angiogenesis, vascular dilatation, and connective tissue hypertrophy. Lung cancer is the most common cause of acquired clubbing. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\">[9]</a></li><li><strong>Tachypnea:</strong> Increased respiratory rate above the normal adult range of 12-20 breaths per minute. Pathogenesis: A compensatory response mediated by the medullary respiratory center to hypoxemia (carotid body chemoreceptors), hypercapnia (central chemoreceptors), acidosis (peripheral and central chemoreceptors), decreased lung compliance (stretch receptors, J-receptors), pain, anxiety, or fever (hypothalamic reset). It is the single most sensitive sign of an impending respiratory crisis (sepsis, pulmonary embolism, heart failure) and is a component of early warning scores (NEWS2). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a></li><li><strong>Use of Accessory Muscles:</strong> Recruitment of the sternocleidomastoid, scalene, trapezius, pectoralis major, and platysma muscles to assist inspiration. Pathogenesis: Normally, quiet inspiration is driven solely by the diaphragm and external intercostals. When the work of breathing is critically elevated (severe airflow obstruction, stiff lungs, profound hyperinflation), the patient unconsciously fixes the shoulder girdle by bracing the arms (orthopneic position, tripod position) to reverse the origin and insertion of these muscles, allowing them to elevate the sternum and upper ribs, increasing thoracic volume. It is a sign of severe respiratory distress and impending respiratory failure. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Paradoxical Abdominal Breathing (Respiratory Alternans):</strong> During inspiration, the abdomen moves inward while the chest moves outward, the opposite of normal. Pathogenesis: Diaphragmatic fatigue or paralysis. A fatigued diaphragm is sucked upward into the thorax during inspiration by the negative intrathoracic pressure generated by the accessory muscles, causing abdominal indrawing. It is a sensitive sign of diaphragmatic dysfunction and a pre-terminal sign in severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) indicating imminent respiratory arrest. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Hyper-resonance:</strong> A percussion note louder, lower-pitched, and longer in duration than the normal resonant note. Pathogenesis: An increased ratio of air to solid tissue within the underlying thoracic cavity. It occurs in conditions of air trapping (emphysema, asthma) where the lungs are hyperinflated but the alveolar walls are intact, or in pneumothorax where air occupies the pleural space. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Dullness to Percussion:</strong> A soft, short, high-pitched percussion note. Pathogenesis: Replacement of normally air-filled lung tissue with fluid (consolidation in pneumonia), solid tissue (tumor, atelectasis), or interposition of fluid between the lung and chest wall (pleural effusion). The extreme form is &quot;stony dullness,&quot; pathognomonic for a large pleural effusion. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Decreased or Absent Breath Sounds:</strong> Reduction or abolition of the normal vesicular breath sounds heard through the stethoscope. Pathogenesis: Three principal mechanisms: (1) impaired sound generation due to reduced airflow velocity (severe bronchospasm, emphysema with loss of elastic recoil, hypoventilation from narcotic overdose or neuromuscular disease); (2) impaired sound transmission due to attenuation by fluid, air, or tissue interposed between the lung and stethoscope (pleural effusion, pneumothorax, thick chest wall, pleural thickening); (3) complete obstruction of the airway (mucus plug, foreign body) preventing air entry. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Bronchial Breathing:</strong> High-pitched, tubular breath sounds heard over the peripheral lung fields during both inspiration and expiration, with a pathological gap (pause) between the two phases. Pathogenesis: Under normal conditions, lung parenchyma filters the high-frequency components of the bronchial breath sounds generated in the trachea and main bronchi, converting them to the low-pitched, rustling vesicular sounds. Consolidation (e.g., lobar pneumonia) solidifies the lung, transforming it into a homogeneous, acoustically dense medium that transmits bronchial sounds from the central airways directly to the chest wall without filtration. This is a classic sign of lobar pneumonia with patent bronchi. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Crackles (Rales, Crepitations):</strong> Discontinuous, brief, explosive, non-musical sounds. They are divided into two pathogenetically distinct categories. Fine end-inspiratory crackles: A series of high-pitched, velcro-like sounds heard in late inspiration. Pathogenesis: The sudden, explosive opening of small, previously collapsed, fluid-obstructed distal airways and alveoli, equalizing pressure differences. This is the hallmark of interstitial pulmonary fibrosis and early pulmonary edema. Coarse early-inspiratory crackles: Lower-pitched, bubbling, gurgling sounds heard in early inspiration, often clearing with coughing. Pathogenesis: Air bubbling through secretions (mucus, pus, edema fluid) in medium-to-large airways. This typifies chronic bronchitis, bronchiectasis, and resolving pneumonia. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Pleural Friction Rub:</strong> A leathery, creaking, grating sound heard during both inspiration and expiration, usually localized to the lower lateral chest, and not cleared by coughing. Pathogenesis: Roughened, inflamed, fibrin-coated parietal and visceral pleural surfaces rubbing against each other during the respiratory cycle. It disappears when a pleural effusion separates the two layers, and it may reappear as the effusion resolves. It is pathognomonic for pleuritis. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Tracheal Deviation:</strong> Displacement of the trachea from the midline, assessed by palpating the space between the tracheal cartilage and the sternocleidomastoid muscle in the suprasternal notch. Pathogenesis: A push indicates a space-occupying lesion on the opposite side (massive pleural effusion, tension pneumothorax, large mediastinal tumor). A pull indicates volume loss or fibrosis on the same side (lobar collapse/atelectasis, pneumonectomy, extensive pleural thickening/fibrothorax). It is a critical sign in the rapid assessment of tension pneumothorax. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Increased Tactile Fremitus:</strong> Palpable vibration of spoken sounds (&quot;99&quot; or &quot;toy boat&quot;) transmitted through the chest wall is increased. Pathogenesis: Sound transmission is maximized through solid or liquid media and minimized through air. Consolidation (pneumonia) enhances the transmission of vocal vibrations from the central bronchi to the periphery, making the fremitus palpable. Conversely, decreased fremitus occurs in pleural effusion, pneumothorax, and emphysema. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Pulsus Paradoxus:</strong> An exaggerated inspiratory fall in systolic blood pressure (&gt;10 mm Hg) during quiet breathing. Pathogenesis: Normally, inspiration reduces intrathoracic pressure, increasing venous return to the right ventricle (RV) but also increasing pulmonary vascular capacitance, which transiently reduces left ventricular (LV) filling. In conditions with severely limited cardiac tamponade (cardiac tamponade), marked airway obstruction with hyperinflation (status asthmaticus, severe COPD), or massive pulmonary embolism, the inspiratory drop in LV stroke volume is amplified, producing a clinically detectable paradox. It is a key bedside sign of life-threatening cardiac tamponade. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Kussmaul Breathing:</strong> Deep, rapid, sighing respirations with a regular rhythm, driven by severe metabolic acidosis. Pathogenesis: Acidemia stimulates peripheral and central chemoreceptors, driving an intense medullary respiratory response. The increased tidal volume and rate aim to reduce PaCO₂, a compensatory respiratory alkalosis to buffer the acute metabolic acidosis. It is most classically seen in diabetic ketoacidosis. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"third-clinical-examination-of-the-respiratory-system","عنوان":"Third: Clinical Examination of the Respiratory System","html":"<p>The standard sequence is Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation (IPPA). The patient is ideally examined sitting upright at approximately 45 degrees, undressed to the waist, with dignity preserved by covering the anterior chest when examining the back. The examination must be performed on bare skin, never through clothing.</p><h3 id=\"inspection\">Inspection</h3><ul><li><strong>General Inspection (End of Bed):</strong> Assess consciousness level, general nutritional status (cachexia of chronic disease, obesity affecting mechanics), comfort at rest (orthopnea, tripod position, inability to speak in full sentences), visible chest wall deformities (barrel chest of emphysema, pectus excavatum/carinatum, kyphoscoliosis), and the presence of ancillary equipment (oxygen cannula, nebulizer mask, peak flow meter, sputum pot, inhalers, chest drain). Observe the respiratory rate silently for a full 60 seconds without informing the patient, as awareness alters the rate. Note the breathing pattern: Cheyne-Stokes (crescendo-decrescendo pattern with apneas, in heart failure, stroke, and at altitude), Biot&#39;s (irregular, ataxic breathing in medullary injury), and Kussmaul breathing. Listen for audible sounds: stridor, wheeze, hoarse voice, gurgling (death rattle), and explosive cough. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a></li><li><strong>Hands and Upper Body:</strong> Inspect the fingers for clubbing (Schamroth&#39;s window test: loss of the diamond-shaped window normally seen when dorsal surfaces of terminal phalanges of thumbs are opposed), peripheral cyanosis (blue fingertips), tar staining (smoking), and CO₂ retention flap (asterixis: coarse flapping tremor of the outstretched, dorsiflexed hands, a sign of severe hypercapnia). Inspect the wrist for a bounding pulse (CO₂ retention causing vasodilation). Inspect the conjunctivae for pallor (anemia), the tongue and lips for central cyanosis, and the face for plethora (polycythemia secondary to chronic hypoxia) or Horner&#39;s syndrome (Pancoast tumor). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Chest Inspection:</strong> Inspect the shape (anteroposterior diameter increased in barrel chest, prominent sternum in pectus carinatum, depressed sternum in pectus excavatum), symmetry of chest wall expansion (asymmetrical expansion suggests unilateral pathology like effusion, pneumothorax, collapse, or splinting from pleuritic pain), and the presence of scars (thoracotomy, chest drain scar) or radiotherapy tattoos. Observe the respiratory movement: the normal male pattern is abdominothoracic (diaphragmatic); the female pattern is thoracoabdominal. Prolonged expiration with pursed-lip breathing (auto-PEEP technique to stent airways open) is characteristic of severe COPD. Inspect for visible dilated chest wall veins (superior vena cava obstruction). Count the respiratory rate exactly. Note the use of accessory muscles (sternocleidomastoid and scalene contractions visible during inspiration). Note intercostal, supraclavicular, and subcostal indrawing (retractions), particularly in infants and in severe adult airway obstruction. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"palpation\">Palpation</h3><ul><li><strong>Tracheal Position:</strong> Use the index and ring fingers of one hand to palpate the sternal heads of each sternocleidomastoid muscle. Place the middle finger gently in the suprasternal notch to determine if the trachea is central or deviated. A lateralized trachea is a life-threatening sign until proven otherwise (tension pneumothorax). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Chest Expansion:</strong> Stand in front of the patient. Place both hands firmly on the anterolateral chest wall, fingers extended around the sides, thumbs meeting in the midline, approximately at the level of the 4th intercostal space (lower border of the pectoralis major). Raise a small skin fold between the thumbs. Ask the patient to take a deep breath in and then exhale fully. Observe the divergence of the thumbs (normally symmetrical, &gt;4 cm divergence). In upper lobe fibrosis or collapse, expansion on the affected side is reduced (apical expansion, hands placed high on the chest, over the upper trapezius). In lower lobe pathology, assess basal expansion by placing hands at the level of the 10th ribs posteriorly, thumbs meeting in the midline. Asymmetrical expansion strongly suggests unilateral pleural or pulmonary disease. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Tactile (Vocal) Fremitus:</strong> Place the ulnar border of the hand or the palmar base of the fingers on the chest wall. Ask the patient to repeat the phrase &quot;ninety-nine&quot; or &quot;toy boat.&quot; Start at the apices and move systematically down, comparing symmetrical points on the right and left hemithorax. The vibration should be felt symmetrically, being slightly more prominent in the right upper lobe (due to the more direct bronchial anatomy) and in the interscapular region (proximity to main bronchi). Increased fremitus indicates consolidation (sound transmitted by solid lung); decreased or absent fremitus indicates pleural effusion, pneumothorax, thick chest wall, or bronchial obstruction. This sign has a sensitivity of 82% and specificity of 79% for detecting pneumonia when combined with other physical signs. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a></li><li><strong>Chest Wall Tenderness and Crepitus:</strong> Palpate the rib cage for focal tenderness (rib fracture, costochondritis, tumor metastasis). Subcutaneous emphysema (surgical emphysema) is detected by a crackling sensation under the fingertips, resembling &quot;Rice Krispies.&quot; This indicates air dissecting from a pneumothorax or pneumomediastinum into the subcutaneous tissue planes. It is an important sign of barotrauma. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"percussion\">Percussion</h3><p>Percussion is performed using the indirect (mediate) method. The middle finger (pleximeter) of the non-dominant hand is pressed firmly against the chest wall in an intercostal space. The tip of the middle finger (plexor) of the dominant hand strikes the distal interphalangeal joint of the pleximeter finger with a loose, snapping wrist motion, withdrawing immediately. Percuss systematically from apex to base, comparing right and left sides. The sequence is: anterior chest (patient supine if possible, or sitting), lateral chest, and posterior chest (patient sitting, arms crossed in front to move scapulae laterally). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></p><h3 id=\"percussion-notes\">Percussion Notes:</h3><ul><li><strong>Resonant:</strong> Normal air-filled lung. Low-pitched, hollow, long duration.</li><li><strong>Hyper-resonant:</strong> Increased air trapping (emphysema, asthma) or pleural air (pneumothorax). Lower-pitched, louder, longer than resonant.</li><li><strong>Dull:</strong> Decreased air content (consolidation, atelectasis, fibrosis, pleural thickening). Soft, high-pitched, short.</li><li><strong>Stony Dull:</strong> Fluid in the pleural space (massive effusion, empyema). No resonance whatsoever; the note is flat and short, like percussing a rock. This is a highly specific sign for pleural fluid.</li><li><strong>Tympanitic:</strong> Gas under pressure in a hollow viscus (gastric bubble, large pneumothorax). Drum-like, musical quality.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Diaphragmatic Excursion:</strong> Percuss to determine the level of the diaphragm posteriorly during quiet breathing and at full inspiration and expiration. Normally, the diaphragm descends 3-5 cm on deep inspiration. Absent or paradoxical excursion (diaphragm rises with inspiration) indicates phrenic nerve palsy or diaphragmatic paralysis. A hyperinflated, flattened diaphragm in emphysema will have low resting position and minimal excursion. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Cardiac and Liver Dullness:</strong> The normal area of cardiac dullness is obliterated in hyperinflation (emphysema), where the overinflated lungs cover the heart. The normal upper border of liver dullness is at the 5th intercostal space in the right midclavicular line; loss of this dullness (tympany percussed over the right lower anterior chest) is a classic sign of a ruptured abdominal viscus with free intraperitoneal air (pneumoperitoneum), not a primary respiratory sign, but must be recognized. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"auscultation\">Auscultation</h3><p>Auscultation is performed using the diaphragm of the stethoscope pressed firmly against the bare skin. The sequence is the same as percussion: apex to base, comparing right to left at each level. The patient breathes deeply through an open mouth. If the patient becomes lightheaded, allow a pause, as deep breathing causes hypocapnia. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></p><h3 id=\"normal-breath-sounds\">Normal Breath Sounds:</h3><ul><li><strong>Vesicular:</strong> Soft, low-pitched, rustling, heard throughout inspiration and into the first third of expiration. Produced by turbulent airflow in the lobar and segmental bronchi filtered by the alveoli.</li><li><strong>Bronchovesicular:</strong> Intermediate pitch and intensity, heard over the main bronchi (manubrium sterni, interscapular area). Inspiration and expiration are about equal in duration.</li><li><strong>Tracheal (Bronchial):</strong> Loud, high-pitched, tubular, heard over the trachea. Expiration is longer than inspiration, with a definite gap between the two phases. Heard peripherally, this is an abnormal finding indicating consolidation. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"adventitious-sounds\">Adventitious Sounds:</h3><ul><li><strong>Crackles:</strong> As described under Signs. Note timing (early vs. late inspiratory), quality (fine vs. coarse), and distribution (bibasilar, unilateral).</li><li><strong>Wheeze:</strong> As described. Note timing (expiratory vs. inspiratory), quality (monophonic vs. polyphonic), and location (localized, diffuse).</li><li><strong>Rhonchi:</strong> Low-pitched, sonorous, continuous sounds that are similar to wheezes but are generated by secretions in larger airways. They typically change or clear with coughing, unlike wheezes. They are essentially coarse crackles overlapping in time.</li><li><strong>Pleural Friction Rub:</strong> As described. A leathery, localized, grating sound. To distinguish from crackles, ask the patient to hold their breath; a rub may disappear or diminish, but it often persists as the pleural surfaces can still rub together from transmitted cardiac motion.</li><li><strong>Stridor:</strong> As described. Auscultate over the neck (larynx and trachea) to localize the source. It is a sign of critical airway narrowing.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Vocal Resonance:</strong> After auscultating breath sounds, assess the transmission of the spoken voice. Ask the patient to say &quot;ninety-nine.&quot; Normally, the sound is muffled and unintelligible through the lung.<ul><li><strong>Bronchophony:</strong> The spoken words are louder and clearer than normal over a consolidated area.</li><li><strong>Whispering Pectoriloquy:</strong> Ask the patient to whisper &quot;ninety-nine.&quot; In consolidation, the whispered numbers are heard clearly and distinctly. In normal lung, the whisper is faint and indistinct. This is a highly specific sign of consolidation, with a specificity of up to 96% when combined with bronchial breathing. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a></li><li><strong>Egophony:</strong> Ask the patient to say &quot;E.&quot; Over a consolidated area or above a pleural effusion, the &quot;E&quot; is transformed into a nasal &quot;A&quot; (like a goat&#39;s bleat). This is a classic sign of lobar pneumonia. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul></li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"fourth-investigations-requested-for-the-respiratory-system","عنوان":"Fourth: Investigations Requested for the Respiratory System","html":"<p>Investigations are ordered in a logical, stepwise manner, starting with simple, non-invasive bedside tests and escalating to complex imaging and invasive procedures as clinically indicated.</p><ul><li><strong>Pulse Oximetry:</strong> A non-invasive, continuous measurement of arterial oxyhemoglobin saturation (SpO₂). The probe uses spectrophotometry, emitting red and infrared light through a pulsatile tissue bed (finger, earlobe). Deoxyhemoglobin absorbs red light (660 nm), and oxyhemoglobin absorbs infrared light (940 nm). The ratio of absorbed light is processed to calculate the percentage of hemoglobin saturated with oxygen. It is a universal screening tool. Limitations: It cannot distinguish oxyhemoglobin from carboxyhemoglobin (falsely normal/high in CO poisoning) or methemoglobin (SpO₂ trends toward 85%). Accuracy drops below SpO₂ 80% and in low perfusion states. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Arterial Blood Gas (ABG):</strong> The gold standard for assessing gas exchange and acid-base status. A sample of arterial blood, most commonly drawn from the radial artery (Allen&#39;s test performed first to ensure ulnar collateral circulation), is analyzed using a blood gas analyzer. Parameters measured: pH, PaCO₂, PaO₂, HCO₃⁻, base excess, and calculated SaO₂. It defines respiratory failure (Type 1: PaO₂ &lt; 8 kPa / 60 mm Hg with normal PaCO₂; Type 2: PaO₂ &lt; 8 kPa with PaCO₂ &gt; 6.5 kPa / 50 mm Hg) and enables classification of acid-base disorders. The A-a gradient [(713 × FiO₂) - (PaCO₂/0.8) - PaO₂] distinguishes hypoventilation (normal A-a) from V/Q mismatch, shunt, or diffusion impairment (elevated A-a). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Peak Expiratory Flow Rate (PEFR):</strong> A portable, bedside measure of the maximum expiratory airflow. The patient takes a maximal inspiration and then blows as hard and fast as possible into the device. It is measured in liters per minute. It primarily reflects large airway caliber and is effort-dependent. It is used for serial monitoring of asthma exacerbations (severity, response to treatment) and establishing diurnal variation (a &gt;20% variability supports a diagnosis of asthma). It is not diagnostic for airflow obstruction on its own; spirometry is required. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Spirometry:</strong> The gold-standard pulmonary function test. The patient performs a maximal forced expiratory maneuver from total lung capacity (TLC) to residual volume (RV). Key parameters: Forced Vital Capacity (FVC) — total volume exhaled forcefully; Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 second (FEV₁) — volume exhaled in the first second; FEV₁/FVC ratio — the single most important number; normal is &gt;0.70 (or below the lower limit of normal, LLN). An obstructive pattern: FEV₁ reduced, FVC relatively preserved, FEV₁/FVC &lt; 0.70. A restrictive pattern: FEV₁ and FVC proportionally reduced, FEV₁/FVC normal or high. Bronchodilator reversibility testing (improvement in FEV₁ by ≥12% and ≥200 mL after salbutamol) diagnoses asthma. Flow-volume loops identify intrathoracic vs. extrathoracic obstruction. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Lung Volumes (Plethysmography):</strong> Body plethysmography or helium dilution tests measure total lung capacity (TLC), functional residual capacity (FRC), and residual volume (RV). It is the definitive test for diagnosing a restrictive ventilatory defect (TLC &lt; 80% predicted) and quantifying hyperinflation (elevated TLC, RV, and RV/TLC ratio) in COPD. It is essential when spirometry suggests restriction to confirm a true lung volume reduction. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Diffusion Capacity for Carbon Monoxide (DLCO):</strong> Measures the ability of the lungs to transfer gas across the alveolar-capillary membrane. The patient inhales a small, safe concentration of carbon monoxide (CO), holds the breath for 10 seconds, and the exhaled CO concentration is measured. Because CO has a very high affinity for hemoglobin, its uptake is limited only by diffusion across the membrane and capillary blood volume. DLCO is reduced in: pulmonary fibrosis (thickened membrane), emphysema (loss of capillary bed), pulmonary hypertension (vascular obliteration), anemia (reduced hemoglobin), and interstitial lung disease. It is normal or elevated in asthma and in intrapulmonary hemorrhage (blood in alveoli sequesters CO). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Chest X-Ray (CXR):</strong> The first-line imaging investigation. Standard views are posteroanterior (PA) and lateral. Assess systematically using the RIP-ABCDE approach: Rotation, Inspiration (count 5-7 anterior and 9-11 posterior ribs), Penetration (thoracic spine just visible behind the heart); Airways (trachea, carina), Breathing (lung fields, fissures), Cardiac (size, silhouette sign), Diaphragm (angles, free air), Everything Else (bones, soft tissues, lines, tubes). The silhouette sign is the loss of a normal radiographic border due to adjacent lung consolidation or collapse. Air bronchograms (air-filled bronchi visible against opacified lung) indicate airspace consolidation, not pleural effusion. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a></li><li><strong>Computed Tomography (CT) of the Chest:</strong> Provides cross-sectional and three-dimensional imaging of the lung parenchyma, airways, pleura, and mediastinum. High-Resolution CT (HRCT) uses thin sections (1-1.5 mm) to provide exquisite detail of the secondary pulmonary lobule, crucial for diagnosing interstitial lung diseases (usual interstitial pneumonia pattern, nonspecific interstitial pneumonia pattern, honeycombing). CT Pulmonary Angiography (CTPA) with intravenous contrast is the gold standard for diagnosing pulmonary embolism, visualizing filling defects in the pulmonary arteries. Standard contrast-enhanced CT stages lung cancer and evaluates mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy. Low-dose CT (LDCT) is the screening modality of choice for lung cancer in high-risk populations. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a></li><li><strong>Ventilation-Perfusion (V/Q) Scan:</strong> A nuclear medicine study involving inhalation of a radioactive gas (ventilation) and intravenous injection of technetium-99m-labeled macroaggregated albumin (perfusion). Mismatched perfusion defects (normal ventilation, absent perfusion) are characteristic of pulmonary embolism. It is most useful in patients with contraindications to CTPA (renal failure, severe contrast allergy) and for assessing differential lung function prior to resectional surgery. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Bronchoscopy:</strong> Direct visualization of the tracheobronchial tree using a flexible fiberoptic or rigid bronchoscope. It allows inspection of the airway mucosa, collection of specimens (bronchoalveolar lavage for microbiology and cytology, bronchial washings, transbronchial biopsy, endobronchial ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) for mediastinal lymph node sampling), and therapeutic interventions (foreign body removal, stent placement, laser ablation). It is central to the diagnosis of lung cancer, interstitial lung disease (if HRCT is non-diagnostic), and pulmonary infections in immunocompromised hosts. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Thoracentesis (Pleural Aspiration):</strong> Insertion of a needle through the chest wall into the pleural space to aspirate fluid for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes. Diagnostic thoracentesis is indicated for all new pleural effusions of unknown etiology. Pleural fluid is analyzed for appearance, protein, lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) (to apply Light&#39;s criteria: exudate if pleural fluid protein/serum protein &gt;0.5 OR pleural fluid LDH/serum LDH &gt;0.6 OR pleural fluid LDH &gt;2/3 upper limit of normal serum LDH), cytology (malignant cells), microbiology (Gram stain, culture, tuberculosis PCR and culture), and pH (pH &lt; 7.2 in parapneumonic effusion is an indication for chest tube drainage). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Tuberculin Skin Test (Mantoux) and Interferon-Gamma Release Assays (IGRA):</strong> Tests for latent tuberculosis infection. Mantoux: intradermal injection of purified protein derivative (PPD) and measurement of induration at 48-72 hours. IGRA (QuantiFERON-TB Gold, T-SPOT.TB): measures interferon-gamma release from T-cells stimulated by M. tuberculosis-specific antigens (ESAT-6, CFP-10) in a blood sample. IGRA is more specific than PPD in BCG-vaccinated populations. Neither distinguishes latent from active disease; diagnosis of active TB requires microbiological confirmation (sputum smear, PCR, culture). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"fifth-assessment-of-respiratory-health-and-screening","عنوان":"Fifth: Assessment of Respiratory Health and Screening","html":"<p>A clinically healthy respiratory system is one that supports adequate gas exchange at rest and on exertion, without symptoms, and with normal structural and functional integrity. Assessment to rule out occult disease involves a combination of history, physical examination, and, in specific high-risk populations, screening tests.</p><ul><li><strong>History-Based Screening (Primary Assessment):</strong> The absence of cough, dyspnea, hemoptysis, chest pain, and wheeze is a strong negative predictor of significant pulmonary disease. However, significant disease can exist asymptomatically, particularly in early-stage lung cancer, early interstitial lung disease, and COPD (patients often unconsciously reduce their activity to avoid dyspnea). Therefore, a detailed smoking history (pack-years = number of packs per day × number of years smoked), occupational and environmental exposure history (asbestos, silica, coal dust, organic antigens, radon), and family history of respiratory disease (alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, cystic fibrosis) must be elicited. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Physical Examination-Based Screening:</strong> A normal respiratory rate (12-20/min), absence of clubbing and cyanosis, symmetrical chest expansion, resonant percussion, and normal vesicular breath sounds with no adventitious sounds essentially rules out clinically significant structural lung disease at the bedside. However, physical examination has poor sensitivity for detecting early-stage disease, particularly interstitial lung disease (fine crackles may be the only sign) and early emphysema. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a></li><li><strong>Pulse Oximetry as a Screening Tool:</strong> Resting SpO₂ is a useful but imperfect screening tool for hypoxemia. A resting SpO₂ of 96-100% is normal. An SpO₂ ≤ 94% at rest in a non-smoker is abnormal and requires investigation with ABG. However, exercise desaturation (a drop in SpO₂ by &gt;4% during a 6-minute walk test) is a far more sensitive indicator of underlying interstitial lung disease or pulmonary vascular disease than resting oximetry. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Spirometry as a Screening Tool:</strong> Spirometry is the cornerstone screening test for COPD in at-risk populations. The Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) and the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) recommend spirometry in adults aged 40-80 years with a smoking history of ≥20 pack-years, or with chronic respiratory symptoms (dyspnea, cough, sputum). A post-bronchodilator FEV₁/FVC &lt; 0.70 confirms persistent airflow limitation (COPD). Screening spirometry is not recommended in asymptomatic adults without risk factors. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></li><li><strong>Low-Dose CT (LDCT) for Lung Cancer Screening:</strong> The U.S. Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) and the National Lung Screening Trial (NLST) established the evidence for LDCT lung cancer screening. The current USPSTF 2021 recommendation is annual screening with LDCT in adults aged 50 to 80 years who have a 20 pack-year smoking history and currently smoke or have quit within the past 15 years. Screening reduces lung cancer mortality by 20%. Harms include a high rate of false positives (∼96% of positive screens are not cancer), overdiagnosis, radiation exposure (∼1.5 mSv per scan), and patient anxiety. Shared decision-making is mandatory. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-14\" data-مصادر=\"14\">[14]</a></li><li><strong>Screening for Alpha-1 Antitrypsin Deficiency:</strong> The American Thoracic Society (ATS) and European Respiratory Society (ERS) recommend screening all adults with COPD, especially those with early-onset emphysema (age &lt;45), emphysema in a non-smoker, predominantly basilar panacinar emphysema on CT, or a strong family history of COPD or liver disease. The test is serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level, with genotype (PI typing) if the level is low. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"sixth-diseases-of-the-respiratory-system","عنوان":"Sixth: Diseases of the Respiratory System","html":"<p>A list of the most important and common diseases, with pathogenesis, typical presentation, and the key distinguishing feature.</p><ul><li><strong>Asthma:</strong> Pathogenesis: Chronic airway inflammation involving eosinophils, mast cells, Th2 lymphocytes, and their cytokines (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13), leading to bronchial hyperresponsiveness and reversible airflow obstruction. Environmental triggers (allergens, viruses, exercise, cold air) cause acute bronchospasm, mucosal edema, and mucus plugging. Typical Presentation: Episodic wheeze, dyspnea, chest tightness, and cough, often worse at night and early morning, triggered by specific exposures. Key Distinguishing Feature: Reversible airflow obstruction on spirometry (increase in FEV₁ by ≥12% and 200 mL after bronchodilator) AND symptom variability; the lung function can normalize completely between attacks. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></li><li><strong>Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD):</strong> Pathogenesis: Long-term inhalation of noxious particles and gases (primarily cigarette smoke) drives chronic inflammation in the airways, parenchyma, and vasculature. This results in two overlapping pathological processes: chronic bronchitis (chronic productive cough due to submucosal gland hypertrophy, goblet cell metaplasia, and mucus hypersecretion) and emphysema (permanent destruction of the alveolar walls distal to the terminal bronchioles due to an imbalance between proteases, especially neutrophil elastase, and antiproteases, especially alpha-1 antitrypsin). Loss of elastic recoil causes dynamic airway collapse during expiration and gas trapping (hyperinflation). Typical Presentation: Progressive exertional dyspnea, chronic productive cough, and recurrent chest infections in a middle-aged or older smoker. Advanced disease features are weight loss, cachexia, barrel chest, and cor pulmonale. Key Distinguishing Feature: Persistent, progressive airflow obstruction on spirometry (FEV₁/FVC &lt; 0.70 post-bronchodilator) that is NOT fully reversible. This is the single defining feature separating COPD from asthma. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></li><li><strong>Lobar Pneumonia:</strong> Pathogenesis: Inhalation of virulent bacteria (most commonly Streptococcus pneumoniae) into the distal airspaces, overwhelming host defenses (mucociliary clearance, alveolar macrophages, surfactant proteins A and D). The organism proliferates in the alveoli, triggering an intense neutrophilic inflammatory response. This exudative fluid consolidates the lung, passing through four classic pathological phases: congestion (24h), red hepatization (2-4d, RBCs and fibrin fill alveoli), gray hepatization (4-8d, fibrinopurulent exudate with degraded neutrophils), and resolution (8d+, exudate is enzymatically degraded and cleared). Typical Presentation: Acute onset of high fever, rigors, pleuritic chest pain, and a productive cough with purulent or rust-colored sputum. Physical examination reveals signs of consolidation (bronchial breathing, crackles, increased fremitus, dull percussion). Key Distinguishing Feature: A lobar airspace opacity with air bronchograms on chest X-ray in a febrile patient. This consolidation is the hallmark. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Tuberculosis (TB):</strong> Pathogenesis: Inhalation of aerosolized droplet nuclei containing Mycobacterium tuberculosis. The bacilli are phagocytosed by alveolar macrophages but survive by inhibiting phagolysosome fusion. They multiply intracellularly, forming the Ghon focus. Infected macrophages drain to the regional lymph node, forming the Ghon complex. Cell-mediated immunity (Th1 cells, IFN-gamma) activates macrophages to form a granuloma (caseating epithelioid granuloma with Langhans giant cells) that contains the bacilli (latent TB infection, LTBI). In a minority (5-10%, higher with HIV), the granuloma breaks down, leading to active, cavitary disease in the apical-posterior lung segments (high oxygen tension favors M. tuberculosis growth). Typical Presentation: Insidious onset of constitutional symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss, anorexia) over weeks to months, with a chronic productive cough, sometimes hemoptysis. Key Distinguishing Feature: Apical-posterior cavitary lesions on chest imaging in a patient with epidemiological risk factors, confirmed by positive sputum smear for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) or positive nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) from sputum. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Pulmonary Embolism (PE):</strong> Pathogenesis: Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) of the lower extremities is the source in &gt;95% of cases. The thrombus embolizes through the right heart to the pulmonary arterial circulation, causing a vascular obstruction. The physiological consequences depend on clot burden and cardiopulmonary reserve: an obstructed vessel creates high V/Q (wasted ventilation), causing hypoxemia. Massive PE obstructs &gt;50% of the pulmonary vascular bed, causing a sudden increase in pulmonary vascular resistance (afterload), leading to acute right ventricular pressure overload, RV dilation and failure, a leftward bowing of the interventricular septum (impairing LV filling), and ultimately obstructive shock and cardiac arrest. Typical Presentation: Acute onset of pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and tachypnea. Massive PE presents with syncope, hypotension, and signs of acute right heart strain (elevated JVP, parasternal heave, loud P2). Key Distinguishing Feature: A high clinical pre-test probability (Wells criteria) with a positive CTPA showing an intraluminal filling defect in a pulmonary artery. The absence of consolidation on CXR despite significant hypoxemia is a clinical clue. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF):</strong> Pathogenesis: A progressive, irreversible fibrotic disease of unknown cause, limited to the lungs, occurring primarily in older adults. The current paradigm is that recurrent micro-injury to the alveolar epithelium in a genetically predisposed host leads to aberrant wound healing. Alveolar epithelial cells undergo apoptosis. Fibroblasts are recruited and differentiate into myofibroblasts, which deposit excessive extracellular matrix (collagen), forming fibroblastic foci, the pathological hallmark. These foci cause architectural distortion and progressive traction bronchiectasis and honeycombing. The TGF-beta pathway is central. Typical Presentation: Gradual onset (over months to years) of progressive exertional dyspnea and a non-productive, dry cough. Examination reveals fine, end-inspiratory &quot;velcro&quot; crackles at the lung bases and clubbing. Key Distinguishing Feature: A definite Usual Interstitial Pneumonia (UIP) pattern on HRCT (bibasilar, subpleural, reticular opacities with honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis without ground-glass, nodules, or consolidation) in the absence of a known cause (drugs, connective tissue disease, hypersensitivity pneumonitis) is diagnostic of IPF without a surgical lung biopsy. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Pneumothorax:</strong> Pathogenesis: Air in the pleural space causes the lung to collapse away from the chest wall. Spontaneous pneumothorax occurs without trauma. Primary spontaneous pneumothorax occurs in young, tall, thin males, due to the rupture of a subpleural bleb (small air pocket just beneath the visceral pleura). Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax occurs in an older patient with underlying lung disease (COPD, cystic fibrosis, interstitial lung disease), where the lung architecture is fragile. Tension pneumothorax is a life-threatening progression where a one-way valve mechanism forms at the site of the pleural rupture, allowing air to enter the pleural space during inspiration but not exit during expiration. This progressively increases intrathoracic pressure, shifting the mediastinum to the opposite side, kinking the great vessels, and obstructing venous return to the heart, leading to cardiac arrest. Typical Presentation: Acute onset of unilateral sharp pleuritic pain and dyspnea. Physical examination reveals absent breath sounds, hyper-resonance to percussion, and decreased chest expansion on the affected side. Tension pneumothorax adds tracheal deviation, distended neck veins, and hypotension. Key Distinguishing Feature: A visible visceral pleural line on upright chest X-ray with absent lung markings peripheral to the line. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA):</strong> Pathogenesis: During sleep, there is a physiological reduction in upper airway dilator muscle tone (genioglossus, tensor palatini). In individuals with predisposing factors (obesity, retrognathia, macroglossia, tonsillar hypertrophy), this relaxation allows the negative intraluminal pressure generated during inspiration to collapse the compliant pharyngeal airway. The collapse causes apnea despite continued respiratory effort. The resultant hypoxia and hypercapnia cause a brainstem arousal (detected on electroencephalogram), which restores pharyngeal muscle tone and airway patency with a loud snort or gasp. This cycle repeats tens to hundreds of times per night, fragmenting sleep, causing sympathetic surges and cardiovascular stress. Typical Presentation: The patient is usually unaware. The history is obtained from the bed partner: loud, habitual snoring interrupted by witnessed apneas terminated by choking or gasping, with excessive daytime sleepiness (measured by Epworth Sleepiness Scale &gt;10). The patient is typically an obese, middle-aged male. Key Distinguishing Feature: An Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI) &gt;5 events per hour on an attended polysomnography (sleep study). No office-based physical exam finding definitively rules OSA in or out. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\">[8]</a></li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"seventh-emergency-conditions-of-the-respiratory-system","عنوان":"Seventh: Emergency Conditions of the Respiratory System","html":"<p>These conditions demand immediate recognition and intervention because they can cause death within minutes.</p><ul><li><strong>Tension Pneumothorax:</strong> Pathogenesis: A one-way valve air leak into the pleural space. Each breath increases intrathoracic pressure, progressively collapsing the ipsilateral lung, pushing the mediastinum to the contralateral side, compressing the contralateral lung, and kinking the superior and inferior vena cava, thereby abolishing venous return. Red Flags: Acute severe dyspnea, distended neck veins, hypotension (shock), tachycardia, tracheal deviation to the opposite side, absent breath sounds and hyper-resonance over the affected hemithorax. How to Detect: This is a clinical diagnosis made at the bedside in seconds. Do NOT delay for a chest X-ray. Immediate needle decompression is performed by inserting a large-bore (14-16 gauge) intravenous cannula into the 2nd intercostal space at the midclavicular line. A gush of air confirms the diagnosis and buys time for a definitive chest drain. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Massive Pulmonary Embolism (High-Risk PE):</strong> Pathogenesis: Sudden obstruction of &gt;50% of the pulmonary arterial tree, causing acute right ventricular failure and obstructive shock. Red Flags: Sudden onset of dyspnea, syncope, hypotension (systolic BP &lt;90 mm Hg for &gt;15 minutes), or cardiac arrest with PEA (pulseless electrical activity). Signs include acute right ventricular heave, loud P2, elevated JVP with a prominent &quot;a&quot; wave. How to Detect: It should be suspected in any patient with unexplained hypotension and evidence of right ventricular strain on ECG (S1Q3T3 pattern, right bundle branch block). Bedside echocardiography shows a dilated, hypokinetic right ventricle. CT pulmonary angiogram confirms the diagnosis immediately if the patient is stable enough to transport. Treatment is immediate systemic thrombolysis unless there are absolute contraindications. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Acute Severe Asthma (Status Asthmaticus):</strong> Pathogenesis: Diffuse, severe bronchospasm causing life-threatening airflow obstruction and dynamic hyperinflation. The patient can barely ventilate, leading to severe hypoxemia, hypercapnia, and exhaustion. Red Flags: Inability to speak in full sentences, a silent chest (too little airflow to generate a wheeze), bradycardia (a pre-terminal sign of severe hypoxia), cyanosis, altered consciousness (drowsiness, confusion), and rising PaCO₂. PEFR is &lt;33% of best or predicted. How to Detect: The combination of severe dyspnea with a silent chest, deteriorating consciousness, and a normal or high PaCO₂ in a patient with asthma (normally, an asthmatic should be hyperventilating and have low PaCO₂). This is life-threatening hypercapnic respiratory failure. Management is immediate high-flow oxygen, continuous nebulized bronchodilators (salbutamol and ipratropium), intravenous corticosteroids, and intravenous magnesium sulfate, with a low threshold for intubation and mechanical ventilation. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS):</strong> Pathogenesis: Diffuse, inflammatory damage to the alveolar-capillary membrane caused by a direct (pneumonia, aspiration) or indirect (sepsis, pancreatitis, trauma) insult. A neutrophil-mediated inflammatory cascade increases capillary permeability, flooding the alveoli with protein-rich edema fluid, inactivating surfactant, and causing diffuse alveolar damage and hyaline membrane formation. The lungs become stiff (low compliance) and profoundly hypoxemic (refractory to supplemental oxygen). Red Flags: Acute onset (within 1 week of a known clinical insult), severe hypoxemia (PaO₂/FiO₂ ratio ≤300 mm Hg for mild, ≤200 for moderate, ≤100 for severe ARDS on PEEP ≥5 cm H₂O), and bilateral opacities on chest imaging not fully explained by cardiac failure. It is rapidly progressive and carries a mortality of 30-40%. How to Detect: Suspect in any critically ill patient who develops rapidly worsening hypoxemia and new bilateral infiltrates on CXR without elevated JVP or other signs of volume overload. Diagnostic criteria are the Berlin Definition. Management is lung-protective ventilation (low tidal volume, 6 mL/kg ideal body weight). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li><li><strong>Upper Airway Obstruction (Stridor):</strong> Pathogenesis: A life-threatening narrowing of the larynx or trachea from angioedema (anaphylaxis, ACE-inhibitor induced), infection (acute epiglottitis, diphtheria, croup), aspiration of a foreign body, or tumor. The airway caliber is critically reduced, leading to turbulent, high-pitched stridor. Red Flags: Acute-onset stridor, drooling (inability to swallow secretions), hoarse voice, tripod position (leaning forward, mouth open, using accessory muscles), and cyanosis. In epiglottitis, a patient with sore throat and a normal oropharyngeal exam who looks toxic has epiglottitis until proven otherwise. How to Detect: Inspect the oropharynx; in epiglottitis in adults, the epiglottis may appear cherry-red and edematous on careful indirect or fiberoptic laryngoscopy, but in children, oropharyngeal examination is contraindicated because it can precipitate complete airway obstruction. This is a &quot;no-delay, get anesthesia and ENT&quot; emergency. Management: Avoid agitating the patient, administer nebulized adrenaline (to buy time), high-flow oxygen, heliox (helium-oxygen mixture to reduce airflow turbulence), and prepare for immediate definitive airway management (intubation, cricothyroidotomy). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"eighth-pharmacological-drug-categories-acting-on-the-respiratory-system","عنوان":"Eighth: Pharmacological Drug Categories Acting on the Respiratory System","html":"<h3 id=\"beta-2-adrenergic-receptor-agonists-β₂-agonists\">Beta-2 Adrenergic Receptor Agonists (β₂-Agonists):</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug Names:</strong> Short-acting (SABA): Salbutamol (albuterol), Terbutaline. Long-acting (LABA): Formoterol, Salmeterol, Vilanterol.</li><li><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> Bind to β₂-adrenergic receptors on airway smooth muscle, activating adenylyl cyclase via Gs protein, which increases intracellular cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP). This activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates myosin light-chain kinase, inhibiting it, and promotes calcium reuptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, causing smooth muscle relaxation and bronchodilation. They also inhibit mast cell mediator release. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"anticholinergics-muscarinic-antagonists\">Anticholinergics (Muscarinic Antagonists):</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug Names:</strong> Short-acting (SAMA): Ipratropium bromide. Long-acting (LAMA): Tiotropium bromide, Umeclidinium, Glycopyrronium, Aclidinium.</li><li><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> Block the M3 muscarinic acetylcholine receptors on airway smooth muscle and submucosal glands. This inhibits vagally-mediated bronchoconstriction (the resting tone of the airways) and reduces mucus secretion. They produce bronchodilation by opposing the bronchoconstrictor action of acetylcholine, the dominant reversible mechanism in COPD. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"inhaled-corticosteroids-ics\">Inhaled Corticosteroids (ICS):</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug Names:</strong> Beclomethasone, Budesonide, Fluticasone propionate, Fluticasone furoate, Ciclesonide, Mometasone furoate.</li><li><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> Bind to the intracellular glucocorticoid receptor (GR). The activated GR complex translocates to the nucleus, where it transrepresses pro-inflammatory transcription factors (NF-κB, AP-1) and transactivates anti-inflammatory genes (lipocortin-1, IL-10). This suppresses the synthesis of cytokines, chemokines, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins, inhibiting the recruitment and activation of eosinophils, mast cells, lymphocytes, and dendritic cells, thereby reducing airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness in asthma. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"leukotriene-receptor-antagonists\">Leukotriene Receptor Antagonists:</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug Names:</strong> Montelukast, Zafirlukast.</li><li><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> Selectively and competitively block the CysLT1 receptor for cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4) on airway smooth muscle, endothelium, and inflammatory cells. This prevents the potent bronchoconstriction, increased vascular permeability, mucus secretion, and eosinophil recruitment mediated by these mast-cell and eosinophil-derived mediators. They are effective in a subset of asthmatics, including those with aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"methylxanthines\">Methylxanthines:</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug Names:</strong> Theophylline, Aminophylline (a theophylline-ethylenediamine complex).</li><li><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> They are non-selective phosphodiesterase (PDE) inhibitors, preventing the breakdown of cAMP and cGMP, leading to smooth muscle relaxation. They also block adenosine receptors (A1 and A2). At low clinical doses, the primary relevant mechanism is likely adenosine receptor antagonism, which prevents adenosine-mediated bronchoconstriction and mast cell activation. They also improve diaphragmatic contractility. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"monoclonal-antibodies-biologics\">Monoclonal Antibodies (Biologics):</h3><h3 id=\"drug-names\">Drug Names:</h3><ul><li>Omalizumab: Humanized monoclonal anti-IgE antibody. Binds free IgE, preventing its binding to the high-affinity FcεRI receptor on mast cells and basophils, thus inhibiting mast cell degranulation.</li><li>Mepolizumab, Reslizumab: Anti-IL-5 monoclonal antibodies. Bind and neutralize IL-5, the key cytokine for eosinophil differentiation, maturation, recruitment, and survival.</li><li>Benralizumab: Anti-IL-5 receptor alpha (IL-5Rα) monoclonal antibody. Binds to the IL-5 receptor on eosinophils and basophils, inducing antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC) and directly depleting eosinophils.</li><li>Dupilumab: Fully human monoclonal antibody against the IL-4 receptor alpha subunit (IL-4Rα), which blocks the signaling of both IL-4 and IL-13, the central Th2 cytokines in type 2 inflammation.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Indications:</strong> Severe, uncontrolled eosinophilic or type 2-high asthma despite maximal standard therapy. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"mucolytics-and-antioxidants\">Mucolytics and Antioxidants:</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug Name:</strong> N-acetylcysteine (NAC).</li><li><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> The free sulfhydryl group in NAC reduces the disulfide bonds in the glycoprotein polymers of mucus, depolymerizing and liquefying viscous, purulent sputum. It also acts as an antioxidant by replenishing intracellular glutathione stores. In COPD, chronic use modestly reduces exacerbation frequency. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li></ul><h3 id=\"alpha-1-antitrypsin-augmentation-therapy\">Alpha-1 Antitrypsin Augmentation Therapy:</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug Name:</strong> Purified pooled human plasma alpha-1 antitrypsin (Prolastin-C, Zemaira).</li><li><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> Weekly intravenous infusions replace the deficient protein in individuals with severe alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and established emphysema. The goal is to raise and maintain serum AAT levels above the protective threshold of 11 µM to inhibit neutrophil elastase and prevent further enzymatic degradation of the lung matrix. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></li></ul><div class=\"كتلة تحقّق\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Verification footer</p><p><strong>Guidelines used:</strong> GINA 2026 Strategy Report and GOLD 2026 Report were confirmed to exist and to be the current documents at the date below [WEB]. The body text of this file was <strong>not</strong> re-derived from them; it is the earlier project document.</p><p><strong>Generated:</strong> 11 September 2026.</p><p><strong>Could not verify — read this before quoting:</strong> the source document carries numbered citation markers but <strong>no bibliography of any kind</strong>. The numbers in square brackets therefore resolve to nothing and must not be presented as sourced. Every quantitative value here is <strong><button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button></strong> until a source is attached.</p><p><strong>Confirm before clinical application:</strong> treat this file as revision notes, not as a referenced document. For any treatment decision go to the primary guideline.</p></div>"}],"مصادر":[{"رقم":1,"نص":"Global Initiative for Asthma. 2026 GINA Strategy Report. Accessed September 2026. <a href=\"https://ginasthma.org/2026-gina-strategy-report/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ginasthma.org</a>"},{"رقم":2,"نص":"Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease. 2026 GOLD Report and Pocket Guide. Accessed September 2026. <a href=\"https://goldcopd.org/2026-gold-report-and-pocket-guide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">goldcopd.org</a>"}]});
